.TH DMENU 1 dmenu\-VERSION
.SH NAME
dmenu \- dynamic menu
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dmenu
.RB [ \-bFfiv ]
.RB [ \-l
.IR lines ]
.RB [ \-m
.IR monitor ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR prompt ]
.RB [ \-P
.IR /helper ]
.RB [ \-fn
.IR font ]
.RB [ \-nb
.IR color ]
.RB [ \-nf
.IR color ]
.RB [ \-sb
.IR color ]
.RB [ \-sf
.IR color ]
.RB [ \-ob
.IR color ]
.RB [ \-of
.IR color ]
.RB [ \-w
.IR windowid ]
.P
.BR dmenu_run " ..."
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dmenu
is a dynamic menu for X, which reads a list of newline\-separated items from
stdin.  When the user selects an item and presses Return, their choice is printed
to stdout and dmenu terminates.  Entering text will narrow the items to those
matching the tokens in the input.
.P
.B dmenu_run
is a script used by
.IR dwm (1)
which lists programs in the user's $PATH and runs the result in their $SHELL.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-b
dmenu appears at the bottom of the screen.
.TP
.B \-F
disables fuzzy matching.
.TP
.B \-f
dmenu grabs the keyboard before reading stdin if not reading from a tty. This
is faster, but will lock up X until stdin reaches end\-of\-file.
.TP
.B \-i
dmenu matches menu items case insensitively.
.TP
.BI \-l " lines"
dmenu lists items vertically, with the given number of lines.
.TP
.BI \-m " monitor"
dmenu is displayed on the monitor number supplied. Monitor numbers are starting
from 0.
.TP
.BI \-p " prompt"
defines the prompt to be displayed to the left of the input field.
.TP
.BI \-P " /helper"
enables asynchronous text previews from the absolute executable path supplied,
requiring an explicit positive decimal
.BR \-l .
The input spans the window above an approximately 40% item column and 60%
preview column. Body height does not shrink with the item count; rows are
clamped to the available screen or embedding window. Unreadable dimensions
are rejected.
.IP
The helper is executed directly, without a shell or additional arguments.
Its stdin receives the unchanged selected label followed by one newline and
then EOF. Its stdout supplies preview text; stderr is discarded.
Treat the helper as trusted local code, not a sandbox. Labels and preview
data are never placed in command arguments, environment variables or diagnostics.
.IP
Selection changes debounce for 40 milliseconds and cancel obsolete work.
The 1000 millisecond monotonic deadline covers execution, stdin delivery,
stdout collection and process completion. Successful output is published only
after stdout EOF and exit status 0. Empty output shows No preview; execution
errors and nonzero exits show Unavailable; deadline expiry shows Timed out.
.IP
The output limit is 65536 bytes. Exactly that many bytes completed successfully
are not truncated. Detecting an additional byte retains the prefix and shows
Truncated even after the menu kills the producer. Loading and terminal states
occupy a fixed footer row; text uses the preceding body rows. With one body
row, a successful preview uses that row and other states display their status.
.IP
Only the latest selected item owns a result. Cancellation sends SIGKILL to the
owned helper group and closes pipes without blocking for exit. Cooperative
descendants are included; descendants intentionally leaving the group are not
contained. Keyboard release and menu exit never wait for helper termination.
.IP
Prepared soft-wrapped text is vertically scrollable.
LF preserves empty and trailing lines; tabs expand to four codepoint-column
stops, reset by LF but not soft wraps. CR, NUL, ESC, other C0/C1 controls,
DEL and invalid UTF-8 bytes become U+FFFD. Terminal escapes are not interpreted.
Valid UTF-8 wraps at codepoint boundaries using the active font metrics;
glyph ink is clipped to the preview row. Full grapheme shaping and glyph
coverage remain limited by Xft and the available fonts.
.IP
Preparation handles at most 256 codepoints per event-loop turn, including
expanded tab spaces, and is cancelled on selection changes. Loading remains
visible until layout is complete. The helper deadline does not include layout.
Display storage is separately bounded to four bytes per retained raw byte plus
one terminator, with at most that many row-offset entries.
Alt-Up/Down scroll one visual row, Alt-PageUp/PageDown scroll one text
viewport, and Alt-Home/End move to the first/last viewport.
Offsets clamp to the last full viewport (or zero when the text fits),
including blank and trailing rows. Only selection changes reset scrolling;
cursor-only edits and redraws retain it. Scrolling does not restart the helper,
prepare text again, edit input or move selection. Existing Ctrl, Alt-letter
and unmodified navigation commands are unchanged.
With one body row, Truncated remains status-only; use at least two body rows
to read truncated text.
Clipboard integration is not yet implemented.
Enter still outputs the original label, never preview content.
Without
.BR \-P ,
the existing menu execution and appearance remain unchanged.
.TP
.BI \-fn " font"
defines the font or font set used.
.TP
.BI \-nb " color"
defines the normal background color.
.IR #RGB ,
.IR #RRGGBB ,
and X color names are supported.
.TP
.BI \-nf " color"
defines the normal foreground color.
.TP
.BI \-sb " color"
defines the selected background color.
.TP
.BI \-sf " color"
defines the selected foreground color.
.TP
.BI \-ob " color"
defines the outline background color (for multiple selection).
.TP
.BI \-of " color"
defines the outline foreground color (for multiple selection).
.TP
.B \-v
prints version information to stdout, then exits.
.TP
.BI \-w " windowid"
embed into windowid.
.SH USAGE
dmenu is completely controlled by the keyboard.  Items are selected using the
arrow keys, page up, page down, home, and end.
.TP
.B Tab
Copy the selected item to the input field.
.TP
.B Return
Confirm selection.  Prints the selected item to stdout and exits, returning
success.
.TP
.B Ctrl-Return
Confirm selection.  Prints the selected item to stdout and continues.
.TP
.B Shift\-Return
Confirm input.  Prints the input text to stdout and exits, returning success.
.TP
.B Escape
Exit without selecting an item, returning failure.
.TP
.B Ctrl-Left
Move cursor to the start of the current word
.TP
.B Ctrl-Right
Move cursor to the end of the current word
.TP
.B C\-a
Home
.TP
.B C\-b
Left
.TP
.B C\-c
Escape
.TP
.B C\-d
Delete
.TP
.B C\-e
End
.TP
.B C\-f
Right
.TP
.B C\-g
Escape
.TP
.B C\-h
Backspace
.TP
.B C\-i
Tab
.TP
.B C\-j
Return
.TP
.B C\-J
Shift-Return
.TP
.B C\-k
Delete line right
.TP
.B C\-m
Return
.TP
.B C\-M
Shift-Return
.TP
.B C\-n
Down
.TP
.B C\-p
Up
.TP
.B C\-u
Delete line left
.TP
.B C\-w
Delete word left
.TP
.B C\-y
Paste from primary X selection
.TP
.B C\-Y
Paste from X clipboard
.TP
.B M\-b
Move cursor to the start of the current word
.TP
.B M\-f
Move cursor to the end of the current word
.TP
.B M\-g
Home
.TP
.B M\-G
End
.TP
.B M\-h
Up
.TP
.B M\-j
Page down
.TP
.B M\-k
Page up
.TP
.B M\-l
